universe u

/-!
# Henselization is compatible with quotients

Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a local ring, let `R → Rh` be a henselization of `R` — henselian local,
ind-étale over `R`, with maximal ideal generated by `𝔪` and residue field `κ` — and let `I ⊆ 𝔪`
be an ideal. This file proves that `Rh/I Rh` is a henselization of `R/I`.

The source deduces this as the case `S = R/I` of Lemma 05WP. The proof given here is direct,
because it is shorter than that specialization and needs none of its quasi-finiteness: each of
the four properties of the henselization of `R/I` is transferred along the quotient maps
separately. `Rh/I Rh` is henselian because a quotient of a henselian local ring by a proper ideal
is henselian (`Stacks.Henselian.henselianLocalRing_quotient`); it is ind-étale over `R/I` because
`Rh/I Rh = Rh ⊗_R R/I` is a cobase change of the ind-étale `R → Rh`, which is Lemma 0BSH
(`CommAlgCat.ind_etale_of_isPushout`) applied to the pushout square of the two quotient maps; the
ideal generated by the image of `𝔪/I` is the maximal ideal by `map_maximalIdeal_quotient`, which
is `hmax` conjugated by `IsLocalRing.map_maximalIdeal_of_surjective`; and the residue field is
unchanged because the two vertical maps of that square are surjective local maps, so induce
isomorphisms on residue fields, and `hres` is the assumption for the top one.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Henselian.HenselizationQuotient.solution`: the statement of Stacks Tag 05WQ.

## Implementation notes

Four facts of independent interest carry the argument, and none of them is in Mathlib.

*A quotient of a local ring by a proper ideal is local*: `isLocalRing_quotient`. Both quotients of
the statement are local by this lemma, `I` being proper because `I ≤ 𝔪`, and `I Rh` because
`I Rh ≤ 𝔪 Rh = 𝔪_{Rh}`.

*A quotient of a henselian local ring by a proper ideal is henselian*:
`Stacks.Henselian.henselianLocalRing_quotient`. A monic `f` over `R/I` lifts
to a monic `g` over `R` of the same degree (`Polynomial.lifts_and_degree_eq_and_monic`, the lift
existing coefficientwise because `R → R/I` is surjective) and the approximate root lifts to some
`r`; there `g(r) ∈ 𝔪_R` because `𝔪_R` is the contraction of `𝔪_{R/I}`, and `g'(r)` is a unit because
`R → R/I` is a local ring map, so a root of `g` congruent to `r` projects to a root of `f` congruent
to the given one.

*A quotient square is a pushout square*:
`Stacks.Henselian.isPushout_quotient_map` says that `R/I → S/IS` is the base
change of `R → R/I` along `R → S`, i.e. `S ⊗_R R/I = S/IS`. It is deduced from
`Ideal.qoutMapEquivTensorQout`, which is the same isomorphism of `S`-modules, by
`Algebra.IsPushout.symm`. It is what feeds Lemma 0BSH for the ind-étale conjunct.

*A surjective local ring map of local rings induces an isomorphism on residue fields*:
`Stacks.Henselian.HenselizationQuotient.residueField_map_bijective_of_surjective`. With it, three of
the four residue field maps of the square are bijective — the two quotient maps by surjectivity and
`R → Rh` by `hres` — and the fourth, the one asked for, is bijective by cancelling a bijection from
the composite.

The maximal ideal of `Rh/I Rh` is the image of that of `R/I`
(`Stacks.Henselian.HenselizationQuotient.map_maximalIdeal_quotient`), by
`IsLocalRing.map_maximalIdeal_of_surjective` on the two quotient maps and `hmax` in between. This is
the third conjunct of the conclusion, and it also supplies the locality of `R/I → Rh/I Rh` through
`IsLocalRing.local_hom_TFAE`.

## References

* [StacksProject 05WQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WQ)
* [StacksProject 05WP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WP), of which the source deduces
  this lemma as the case `S = R/I`
* [StacksProject 0BSH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BSH), stability of ind-étale under
  cobase change
* [StacksProject 04GN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GN), the four properties spelling
  out a henselization
-/



namespace Stacks.Henselian.HenselizationQuotient

open IsLocalRing TensorProduct

/-- If `R → S` is a local ring map with `𝔪_R S = 𝔪_S`, then the ideal of `S/IS` generated by the
image of the maximal ideal of `R/I` is the maximal ideal of `S/IS`. -/
private theorem map_maximalIdeal_quotient {R S : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [IsLocalRing R]
    [IsLocalRing S] [Algebra R S] {I : Ideal R} [IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I)]
    [IsLocalRing (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S))]
    (hmax : (maximalIdeal R).map (algebraMap R S) = maximalIdeal S) :
    (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S))) =
      maximalIdeal (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S)) := by
  have hsq : (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (S ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R S))).comp (Ideal.Quotient.mk I) =
      (Ideal.Quotient.mk (I.map (algebraMap R S))).comp (algebraMap R S) := by
    ext x; rfl
  rw [← map_maximalIdeal_of_surjective (Ideal.Quotient.mk I) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective,
    Ideal.map_map, hsq, ← Ideal.map_map, hmax,
    map_maximalIdeal_of_surjective (Ideal.Quotient.mk (I.map (algebraMap R S)))
      Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective]

/-- The quotient map `A → A/J` onto a local ring is local and induces a bijection on residue
fields. -/
private theorem quotient_mk_isLocalHom_and_residueField_map_bijective {A : Type*} [CommRing A]
    [IsLocalRing A] (J : Ideal A) [IsLocalRing (A ⧸ J)] :
    ∃ _ : IsLocalHom (algebraMap A (A ⧸ J)),
      Function.Bijective (ResidueField.map (algebraMap A (A ⧸ J))) :=
  have : IsLocalHom (algebraMap A (A ⧸ J)) :=
    IsLocalHom.of_surjective _ Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  ⟨this, Stacks.StrictHenselization.QuotientDescent.residueField_map_bijective_of_surjective _
    Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective⟩

/-- **Henselization is compatible with quotients** (Stacks 05WQ). Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a local ring
and let `R → Rh` be a henselization of `R`. Let `I ⊆ 𝔪` be an ideal. Then `Rh/I Rh` is a
henselization of `R/I`. -/
public theorem solution {R Rh : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing Rh]
    [IsLocalRing R] [Algebra R Rh] [HenselianLocalRing Rh] [IsLocalHom (algebraMap R Rh)]
    (hind : (CommAlgCat.etale.{u} R).ind.{u} (CommAlgCat.of R Rh))
    (hmax : (maximalIdeal R).map (algebraMap R Rh) = maximalIdeal Rh)
    (hres : Function.Bijective (ResidueField.map (algebraMap R Rh)))
    (I : Ideal R) (hI : I ≤ maximalIdeal R) :
    letI : Algebra (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) :=
      Ideal.Quotient.algebraQuotientOfLEComap Ideal.le_comap_map
    ∃ (_ : IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I)) (_ : IsLocalRing (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))
        (_ : IsLocalHom (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))),
      HenselianLocalRing (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) ∧
      (CommAlgCat.etale.{u} (R ⧸ I)).ind.{u}
        (CommAlgCat.of (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh))) ∧
      (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh))) =
        maximalIdeal (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) ∧
      Function.Bijective
        (ResidueField.map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))) := by
  have hIne : I ≠ ⊤ := ne_top_of_le_ne_top (maximalIdeal.isMaximal R).ne_top hI
  have hJle : I.map (algebraMap R Rh) ≤ maximalIdeal Rh := hmax ▸ Ideal.map_mono hI
  have hJne : I.map (algebraMap R Rh) ≠ ⊤ :=
    ne_top_of_le_ne_top (maximalIdeal.isMaximal Rh).ne_top hJle
  have hlocR : IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I) := isLocalRing_quotient hIne
  have hlocRh : IsLocalRing (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) := isLocalRing_quotient hJne
  obtain ⟨hmkR, hbijR⟩ := quotient_mk_isLocalHom_and_residueField_map_bijective I
  obtain ⟨hmkRh, hbijRh⟩ :=
    quotient_mk_isLocalHom_and_residueField_map_bijective (I.map (algebraMap R Rh))
  have hQ : (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh))) =
      maximalIdeal (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) := map_maximalIdeal_quotient hmax
  -- the locality of `R/I → Rh/I Rh`: it carries `𝔪_{R/I}` into `𝔪_{Rh/I Rh}` by `hQ`
  have hlh : IsLocalHom (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh))) :=
    ((local_hom_TFAE (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))).out 3 1).mp hQ.le
  -- `Rh/I Rh` is the cobase change of `Rh` along `R → R/I`, so it is ind-étale over `R/I`
  have hpo : Algebra.IsPushout R (R ⧸ I) Rh (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) :=
    Stacks.Henselian.isPushout_quotient_map I
  -- the residue field maps of three sides of that square are bijective, hence so is the fourth
  have hfun : ⇑(ResidueField.map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))) ∘
        ⇑(ResidueField.map (algebraMap R (R ⧸ I))) =
      ⇑(ResidueField.map (algebraMap Rh (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))) ∘
        ⇑(ResidueField.map (algebraMap R Rh)) := by
    refine funext fun y => ?_
    obtain ⟨x, rfl⟩ := residue_surjective y
    change ResidueField.map _ (ResidueField.map (algebraMap R (R ⧸ I)) (residue R x)) =
      ResidueField.map _ (ResidueField.map (algebraMap R Rh) (residue R x))
    rw [ResidueField.map_residue, ResidueField.map_residue, ResidueField.map_residue,
      ResidueField.map_residue]
    rfl
  exact ⟨hlocR, hlocRh, hlh, Stacks.Henselian.henselianLocalRing_quotient hJne,
    CommAlgCat.ind_etale_of_isPushout R (R ⧸ I) Rh (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) hind, hQ,
    (Function.Bijective.of_comp_iff _ hbijR).mp (hfun ▸ hbijRh.comp hres)⟩

end Stacks.Henselian.HenselizationQuotient

/-!
# Henselization is compatible with quotients

Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a local ring, let `R → Rh` be a henselization of `R`, and let `I ⊆ 𝔪` be an
ideal of `R`. Then the quotient `Rh/I Rh` of the henselization is a henselization of the quotient
`R/I`: it is a henselian local ring, it is a filtered colimit of étale `R/I`-algebras, the ideal
generated by the image of the maximal ideal of `R/I` is its maximal ideal, and its residue field
is again `κ`.

Both the hypothesis "`Rh` is a henselization of `R`" and the conclusion "`Rh/I Rh` is a
henselization of `R/I`" are spelled by the four properties of Lemma 04GN, which by the
uniqueness of Lemma 08HT pin a henselization down up to a unique isomorphism over the base. So
this lemma transfers that four-item package along the quotient maps `R → R/I` and
`Rh → Rh/I Rh`.

## Main results

* `IsLocalRing.henselization_quotient`: the statement above. The registered form of Stacks 05WQ is
  `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.quotient_of_le_maximalIdeal`, stated for the predicate
  `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization`.

## Implementation notes

*A henselization is the package of Lemma 04GN.* The hypotheses `HenselianLocalRing Rh`, `hind`,
`hmax`, `hres` (together with the `Algebra R Rh` and `IsLocalHom` binders) are, conjunct for
conjunct, the items (1)–(4) of `IsLocalRing.exists_henselization`, and the conclusion is the same
package for `R/I → Rh/I Rh`; "`Λ` is a filtered colimit of étale `A`-algebras" is therefore
`(CommAlgCat.etale A).ind (CommAlgCat.of A Λ)` here as well. The same statement for the
predicate `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization` of Definition 04GQ, where the hypotheses and the
conclusion are one instance each and the base and the henselization live in independent
universes, is `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.quotient_of_le_maximalIdeal`, which carries the
registration of Stacks 05WQ.

*The `R/I`-algebra structure on `Rh/I Rh`.* It is the one induced by `R → Rh`, namely
`Ideal.Quotient.algebraQuotientOfLEComap Ideal.le_comap_map`, and
`algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I Rh) (Ideal.Quotient.mk I x) = Ideal.Quotient.mk _ (algebraMap R Rh x)`
holds by `rfl`. So the square formed by the two quotient maps and `R → Rh` commutes by
construction and carries no compatibility hypothesis.

*The existentially bound instances.* `IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I)`, `IsLocalRing (Rh ⧸ I Rh)` and the
locality of `R/I → Rh/I Rh` are part of the conclusion, not assumptions, and they are bound ahead
of the conjuncts because `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal` and `IsLocalRing.ResidueField.map` take them
as instance arguments. All three are `Prop`s, so binding them existentially chooses no data; this
is the device used in the statement of Lemma 04GN.

*The hypothesis on `I`.* `I ≤ maximalIdeal R` is the source's `I ⊂ 𝔪_R`, and for a local ring it
is equivalent to `I ≠ ⊤` (`IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal`). It cannot be dropped: for `I = ⊤` both
quotients are the zero ring and none of the conjuncts holds.

Faithfulness to [StacksProject 05WQ], point by point. (1) No hypothesis is dead: each of
`HenselianLocalRing Rh`, `hind`, `hmax`, `hres` is used by the conjunct of the conclusion carrying
the same item number, the `IsLocalHom` binder by the locality of the induced map, and `hI` by the
two `IsLocalRing` conjuncts. (2) The scalars and the base are the source's: the ideal one divides
by is `I Rh = I.map (algebraMap R Rh)`, the ideal of `Rh` generated by the image of `I`, and
`Rh/I Rh` is treated as an algebra over `R/I` — not over `R`, and not over `R` modulo the
contraction of `I Rh` — so the colimit, the maximal ideal and the residue field map of the
conclusion are all over the base `R/I`. (3) The canonical map is pinned: the structure map of
`Rh/I Rh` over `R/I` is the induced quotient map (`rfl`-equal to `Ideal.quotientMap` along
`algebraMap R Rh`), and the last two conjuncts speak of `Ideal.map` and of
`IsLocalRing.ResidueField.map` along that very map, so no unrelated isomorphism of `κ` with the
residue field of `Rh/I Rh` satisfies them. (4) The instantiation is not degenerate: a henselian
local ring is its own henselization, so the hypotheses are satisfied by `R = Rh = K` for a field
`K` (`Field.henselian`, and `K` is étale over `K`, hence ind-étale by
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.le_ind`), with `I = ⊥` the only ideal allowed by `hI`; and the
conclusion is not automatic: for `R = ℤ` localized at `(p)`, whose henselization `Rh` is a proper
subring of `ℤ_[p]`, and `I = p² R`, it asserts that `Rh/p² Rh = ℤ/p²` is a henselization of
`R/p² R = ℤ/p²`, so that both the presentation as a filtered colimit of étale algebras and the
residue field survive the quotient.

## References

* [StacksProject 05WQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WQ)
* [StacksProject 04GN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GN), the four properties of a
  henselization, and `IsLocalRing.exists_henselization`
* [StacksProject 08HT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08HT), which shows those four
  properties pin a henselization down up to a unique isomorphism over the base
* [StacksProject 05WP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WP), of which the source deduces
  this lemma as a special case
-/



@[expose] public section

open IsLocalRing in
/-- **Henselization is compatible with quotients** (Stacks 05WQ). Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a local
ring and let `R → Rh` be a henselization of `R`, that is: `Rh` is a henselian local ring, it is a
filtered colimit of étale `R`-algebras, `𝔪 Rh` is its maximal ideal, and its residue field is
`κ`. Let `I ⊆ 𝔪` be an ideal. Then `Rh/I Rh` is a henselization of `R/I`, for the induced
`R/I`-algebra structure: it is a henselian local ring, it is a filtered colimit of étale
`R/I`-algebras, the ideal generated by the image of `𝔪/I` is its maximal ideal, and its residue
field is again `κ`. -/
public theorem IsLocalRing.henselization_quotient {R Rh : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing Rh]
    [IsLocalRing R] [Algebra R Rh] [HenselianLocalRing Rh] [IsLocalHom (algebraMap R Rh)]
    (hind : (CommAlgCat.etale.{u} R).ind.{u} (CommAlgCat.of R Rh))
    (hmax : (maximalIdeal R).map (algebraMap R Rh) = maximalIdeal Rh)
    (hres : Function.Bijective (ResidueField.map (algebraMap R Rh)))
    (I : Ideal R) (hI : I ≤ maximalIdeal R) :
    letI : Algebra (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) :=
      Ideal.Quotient.algebraQuotientOfLEComap Ideal.le_comap_map
    ∃ (_ : IsLocalRing (R ⧸ I)) (_ : IsLocalRing (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))
        (_ : IsLocalHom (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))),
      HenselianLocalRing (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) ∧
      (CommAlgCat.etale.{u} (R ⧸ I)).ind.{u}
        (CommAlgCat.of (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh))) ∧
      (maximalIdeal (R ⧸ I)).map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh))) =
        maximalIdeal (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)) ∧
      Function.Bijective
        (ResidueField.map (algebraMap (R ⧸ I) (Rh ⧸ I.map (algebraMap R Rh)))) :=
    by apply Stacks.Henselian.HenselizationQuotient.solution <;> assumption

end
